    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0037",
      "sequence": 49,
      "meaning": "The seven narrative books are the lovely flights of steps that delight the mind when viewed with the eyes of knowledge. Raghupati’s unconditioned, unobstructed greatness, which I shall describe, is the lake’s excellent, unfathomable water. Rāma and Sītā’s fame is water like nectar, and the delightful play of comparisons its waves. Dense, beautiful caupāīs are lotus leaves, while lovely poetic devices are gems and shining shells. Chhands, soraṭhās and beautiful dohās form its radiant families of many-coloured lotuses. Unequalled meanings, fine sentiments and beautiful language are its pollen, nectar and fragrance. Accumulations of merit are lovely rows of bees; knowledge, detachment and reflection are swans. Varieties of sound, verbal resonance and poetic quality are its many kinds of enchanting fish. The four aims—wealth, dharma, desire and liberation—and the topics of knowledge and realization are further creatures; the nine rasas, repetition, austerity, yoga and detachment are all beautiful aquatic life in the lake. Meritorious saints’ singing of the name and virtues are its varied waterbirds. Assemblies of saints surround it on all four sides, and faith is sung as its spring season. Many modes of explaining devotion, along with forgiveness, compassion and restraint, are its spreading vines. Equanimity, self-control and observances are flowers; knowledge is fruit, and love for Hari’s feet the nectar celebrated by the Veda. Its many other stories and episodes are parrots, cuckoos and birds of many colours. Spiritual thrill forms orchards, gardens and forests, and joy is the delightful birds’ play. Pure-minded gardeners water them with the water of love flowing from beautiful eyes.",
