    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0038",
      "sequence": 50,
      "meaning": "Those who attentively sing this narrative are the skilful guardians of this lake. Men and women who always listen reverently are godlike and qualified for the Mānasa. But the thoroughly wicked and sensual, like cranes and crows, are unfortunate and do not approach this lake. Snails, frogs and waterweed have no place here, for there are no sensual tales with their many flavours. Therefore lustful crows and cranes lose heart before coming. Reaching this lake is exceedingly difficult; without Rāma’s grace one cannot arrive. Harsh bad company and terrifying wrong paths impede the way; the words of such people are tigers, lions and snakes. The many entanglements of household affairs are very inaccessible, enormous mountains. Dense and difficult forests are delusion, intoxication and pride, while many dreadful rivers are false arguments. Those without provisions of faith and without saints as companions find the Mānasa utterly inaccessible, for Raghunātha is not dear to them.",
