    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0039",
      "sequence": 51,
      "meaning": "Even if someone reaches it after hardship, sleep and feverish chill seize him upon arrival. The terrible cold of inertia enters his heart; though he has come, the unfortunate person cannot bathe. Unable to bathe in or drink from the lake, he returns with his pride intact. If someone later comes to ask him, he explains by maligning the lake. No obstacle besets the one upon whom Rāma looks with gracious favour. Such a person reverently bathes in the lake and is not burned by the three supremely terrible afflictions. Those whose feeling for Rāma’s feet is good never abandon this lake. Whoever wishes to bathe in it, brother, should wholeheartedly seek holy company. Desiring to behold such a Mānasa with the mind’s eyes, the poet’s understanding entered it and became pure. Joy and ardour arose in his heart; a stream of love and delight surged forth. Then flowed the lovely river of poetry, filled with the pure water of Rāma’s fame. Its name is Sarayū, root of great auspiciousness, and the beautiful views of world and Veda are its banks. This holy river, daughter of the excellent Mānasa, uproots the grass and trees of Kali’s impurity. Three kinds of listeners—village, town and city communities—line both banks, while the incomparable assembly of saints is Ayodhyā, root of every auspiciousness.",
