      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0040",
      "sequence": 52,
      "meaning": "The lovely Sarayū of fair fame goes and meets the divine river Gaṅgā of devotion to Rāma. The purifying fame of Rāma’s battles with his younger brother joins them as the beautiful great river Soṇa. Between the two shines the divine stream of devotion together with fine detachment and reflection. This threefold confluence removes the fear of the three afflictions and flows toward the ocean of Rāma’s essential form. Originating in the Mānasa, it meets the divine Gaṅgā and will purify the minds of good people who hear it. Here and there the narrative’s wondrous divisions are like forests and gardens along both banks. Umā and Maheśa’s wedding and procession are innumerable aquatic creatures of many kinds. The joy and celebration of Raghubara’s birth are enchanting whirlpools and waves. The childhood deeds of all four brothers are abundant many-coloured lotuses; the king, queens and household, together with their merits, are bees and waterbirds.",
